" class="sc-gtJxSR cOjBdh pf-70_"><h3 data-pf-type="Heading2" class="sc-eIceNc cJLiOo pf-71_ pf-heading-1-h3 pf-hide">#3. Bedroom Performance Depends on More Than Testosterone Alone.</h3><p class="sc-fWORJz ekBzGm pf-73_ pf-text-1" data-pf-type="Paragraph4"><span class="pf-paragraph-content ">Here's something worth understanding.<br><br>Sexual function isn't controlled by one hormone or one part of your body.<br><br>It involves several interacting systems:&nbsp;<br><br>Hormones influence sexual desire and other aspects of sexual health.<br><br>Blood vessels and circulation play an important role in erections.Your nervous system coordinates arousal and physical responses.<br><br>Your brain processes desire, focus, and psychological stress.Your overall health, sleep, and energy can affect how you feel.And these factors don't operate independently.<br><br>For example, a man can have testosterone levels within the normal range and still experience erectile difficulties.<br><br>Another man may have low sexual desire related to stress, poor sleep, medication, or a hormonal condition.<br><br>The important question isn't always, "How do I increase my testosterone?"<br><br>It's, "What else could be contributing to the changes I'm experiencing?"<br><br>That distinction matters because different causes require different approaches.<br><br>And it explains why focusing exclusively on testosterone may not address every aspect of male sexual health.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sc-ktJcvw lknomy pf-76_ pf-r pf-c-cm pf-r-eh" style="--s-xs:8px;--s-sm:15px;position:relative" data-pf-type="Row"><div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs:12;--c-sm:6;--c-md:6;--c-lg:5"><div data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-gtJxSR cOjBdh pf-77_"><h3 data-pf-type="Heading2" class="sc-eIceNc cJLiOo pf-78_ pf-heading-1-h3 pf-lg-hide pf-md-hide pf-sm-hide"><strong>#4. Your Morning Coffee Can Be More Than Just Caffeine.</strong></h3><img alt="2.png__PID:acda2bbe-ead2-448e-ba1d-3bbda5b97989" src="https://cdn.sho